.\" Covers both commands this package contributes. mc-restore.1 is a .so stub
.\" pointing here; build.sh turns it into a symlink when it compresses.
.TH MC-BACKUP 1 "" "mc" "mc manual"
.SH NAME
mc-backup \- back up and restore an mc-managed Minecraft server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mc backup
.br
.B mc restore
.I ARCHIVE
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B mc-backup
package adds
.B mc backup
and
.B mc restore
to
.BR mc (1),
and a systemd timer that takes a backup on the schedule in
.BR mc-config (5).
.PP
An archive is a gzipped tar of
.I /opt/minecraft
\(em the world, the jar, the mods, the configuration. Both commands need root
and both take the mc lock, so a backup cannot run halfway through an install,
and a restore cannot run while a scheduled backup is archiving the directory it
is emptying.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B mc backup
Archive the server now, into
.IR /var/backups/minecraft/minecraft-YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS.tar.gz ,
UTC. Then rotate: the oldest archives beyond
.B backup.keep
are removed.
.IP
If the server is running and a console plugin is installed, the world is
flushed and held still for the duration \(em see
.BR mc-rcon (1).
Without one the archive is still taken, of a world that was not flushed, and
says so. A backup of an unflushed world beats no backup.
.IP
A half-written archive is deleted rather than left behind, because rotation
would otherwise count it as the newest and a restore could choose it.
.TP
.B mc restore \fIARCHIVE\fR
Replace
.I /opt/minecraft
with the contents of
.IR ARCHIVE .
The server is stopped if it is running, and left stopped: start it yourself
once you have looked at what you restored.
.IP
The archive is validated in full
.I before
the server is stopped and before a single file is removed, so a bad archive is
refused while the running server and its directory are still intact.
Ownership is asserted from the account that exists on this machine rather than
taken from the uids stored in the archive.
.SH "WHAT IS ARCHIVED"
Everything under
.I /opt/minecraft
except
.IR logs/ ,
.I crash-reports/
and
.IR cache/ ,
which are regenerated and are the bulk of a long-lived server's disk use.
.PP
.I mods/
and
.I libraries/
.I are
archived, even though they could in principle be re-downloaded: a restore is a
plain extraction with no network step, and a modpack whose upstream has moved
on is exactly the case a backup exists for.
.PP
Symlinks inside the server directory are archived as symlinks rather than
followed.
.SH SECURITY
An archive is attacker-controlled input handled as root \(em it may have come
from anywhere \(em so a restore validates every member before extracting
anything:
.IP \(bu 2
Names may not escape the destination:
.IR .. ,
absolute paths and symlink traversal are all refused.
.IP \(bu 2
Entry
.I types
are checked as well as names. A hardlink to
.I /etc/shadow
passes every name check and then meets
.BR "chown -R" ;
symlinks, hardlinks, device nodes and FIFOs are refused outright.
.PP
Archives are written by root into a
.B root:root 0700
directory, mode
.BR 0600 ,
and are never handed to the service account. That account runs untrusted mods:
owning the directory would let a compromised server pre-create the next
predictable archive name as a symlink for root's writer to follow, or rewrite
what a later restore extracts as root.
.SH "THE TIMER"
.B minecraft-backup.timer
runs
.B mc backup
on
.BR backup.schedule .
Change the schedule in
.BR mc-config (5);
it is applied at the next
.B mc install
or
.BR "mc upgrade" ,
which regenerates the drop-in.
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl list\-timers minecraft\-backup.timer
systemctl start minecraft\-backup.service   # run one now
journalctl \-u minecraft\-backup.service
.RE
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo mc backup
ls \-l /var/backups/minecraft/
sudo mc restore /var/backups/minecraft/minecraft\-20260810\-020000.tar.gz
sudo mc start
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/backups/minecraft/
Archives.
.BR "root:root 0700" ,
each archive
.BR 0600 .
.TP
.I /etc/minecraft/config.toml
.B backup.keep
and
.BR backup.schedule .
See
.BR mc-config (5).
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/minecraft-backup.timer.d/schedule.conf
Generated drop-in carrying
.BR OnCalendar= .
.TP
.I /run/minecraft/mc.lock
Held for the duration of a backup or a restore.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mc (1),
.BR mc-config (5),
.BR mc-rcon (1),
.BR mc-plugins (5),
.BR systemd.timer (5)
